[Printed, Appendix, p. 160] "PROOF OF RESULT 1: Call a recruitment-induced subgraph $G_S = (V_S, E_S)$ compatible with the observed data if $V_S = V_R$, $\{i, j\} \in E_R$ implies $\{i, j\} \in E_S$, and $\sum_{j 
eq i} 1\{\{i, j\} \in E_S\} \leq d_i$ for each $i \in V_R$. Call an augmented recruitment-induced subgraph $G_{SU} = (V_{SU}, E_{SU})$ compatible with the observed data if conditions 1, 3, and 4 of definition 3 hold. [...] Let $G^1_S$ be the recruitment-induced subgraph obtained by removing any unsampled vertices (and edges connected to them) from $G^1_{SU}$. Define $G^2_S$ in the same way. Clearly both $G^1_S$ and $G^2_S$ are compatible with the observed data under the conditions of definition 3, but $\{i, j\} 
otin G^1_S$ and $\{i, j\} \in G^2_S$." [Corrected sentence] "Clearly both $G^1_S$ and $G^2_S$ are compatible with the observed data in the sense defined for recruitment-induced subgraphs at the opening of this proof, but $\{i, j\} 
otin G^1_S$ and $\{i, j\} \in G^2_S$." [Formal content] Let $G_{SU} = (V_{SU}, E_{SU})$ satisfy conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3, and let $G_S$ be obtained from it by removing the unsampled vertices and the edges connected to them. Then: (a) $G_S$ is compatible in the sense of the opening of the proof: $V_S = V_R$, $\{i, j\} \in E_R$ implies $\{i, j\} \in E_S$, and $\sum_{j 
eq i} 1\{\{i, j\} \in E_S\} \leq d_i$ for each $i \in V_R$; (b) $G_S$ satisfies the degree condition 4 of Definition 3, $d_i = \sum_{j} 1\{\{i, j\} \in E_S\}$ for every $i \in V_R$, if and only if no vertex of $V_R$ has an unsampled neighbour in $G_{SU}$, that is, if and only if $G_{SU} = G_S$. [Instance in which the printed reference fails, from the validator] $V_R = \{1, 2\}$, $M = \{1, 2\}$, $E_R = \emptyset$, $\mathbf{d}_R = (1, 1)$. $G^1_{SU}$: vertices $1, 2, u$, edges $\{1, u\}$, $\{2, u\}$; $G^2_{SU}$: vertices $1, 2$, edge $\{1, 2\}$. Both satisfy conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3 (this is the construction of the proof with $i = 1$, $j = 2$). $G^1_S$ has no edge: its degrees are $(0, 0) 
eq (1, 1)$, so it does not satisfy condition 4 of Definition 3, while it is compatible in the sense of the opening of the proof.
